          "Lectures in Italian can be long and elaborate, with subordinate clauses and Latinate words. Listen for the main verb of each sentence; the clauses around it are often background that you can summarize later.",
          "Italian lecturers often begin with a long introduction that places the topic in history or theory. Do not panic if the first minutes seem general: note the key terms and wait for the announcement of the plan, often introduced by \"vedremo\", \"nella prima parte\" or \"partiamo da\".",
